{"author":{"id":"1d5f412cdeafe9987ad9876b0995bf5032718543d34c038a50cdb41808e9874c"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1788305130,"nonce":"4pccXuZSVyhcvut0Ea7I+KkVuGw=","metadata":{"github-id":"I_kwDOULVc1M8AAAABPPhhKg","github-url":"https://github.com/kanya-approve/torram-ng/issues/12","origin":"github"},"title":"The final partial piece is only handled by accident","message":"The last piece of a torrent is shorter than `piece_length`. `check_file_chunk()` always requests `piece_length` bytes and relies on `read()` returning short at EOF.\n\nThat happens to give the right answer only when the candidate file is exactly the size of the torrent file *and* the piece in question is the last of the whole torrent. It is wrong when:\n\n- the candidate is a preallocated partial (padded out to full size, so the read returns real `piece_length` bytes of mostly-zero data)\n- the candidate is larger than the torrent file\n- the file is not the last one in a multi-file torrent\n\nThe correct length should be computed from the torrent (`total_length - piece_index * piece_length`, clamped), never inferred from EOF.","files":null}]}